{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1375 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "aaed79fad1bafbfdbb0e4e675c0636a8aeeb3207075453bf8c116ad8afca217e" :edb/db-before-t 1374 :edb/db-after-t 1375 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534687 50 #inst "2026-10-08T04:13:43.660Z" 13194139534687 true] [17592186087489 1100 "Consider the circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin, as shown below. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$ that traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(2,0).$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039602/assets/156d160267551e9dbf4db42b14b1e0dbb46ab658a836a760cf31a42eb89662d3.png)" 13194139534687 true] [17592186087489 1100 "Consider the circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin, as shown below. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$ that traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(2,0).$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/assets/156d160267551e9dbf4db42b14b1e0dbb46ab658a836a760cf31a42eb89662d3.png)" 13194139534687 false] [17592186087551 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039602/assets/f44fad12ad1f7d465423f92258caae38e0c536153ba2c3c3a6de573061c69993.png)\n\nThe quarter-circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx = 6\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = 6\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta?$" 13194139534687 true] [17592186087551 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/assets/f44fad12ad1f7d465423f92258caae38e0c536153ba2c3c3a6de573061c69993.png)\n\nThe quarter-circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx = 6\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = 6\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta?$" 13194139534687 false] [17592186087551 1145 "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\quad y = r\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r = 6.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=6\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=6\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-circle, we associate the parameter $\\theta$ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039602/assets/4bd384e2b1a9d0568617ddbfd5ac7dbe9630882b45c40d7912f4809a6e0be551.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta$ to $\\left[0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right],$ we get the required quarter-circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-circle are \n\n$$\nx=6\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=6\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in \\left[0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right].\n$$" 13194139534687 true] [17592186087551 1145 "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\quad y = r\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r = 6.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=6\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=6\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-circle, we associate the parameter $\\theta$ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/assets/4bd384e2b1a9d0568617ddbfd5ac7dbe9630882b45c40d7912f4809a6e0be551.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta$ to $\\left[0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right],$ we get the required quarter-circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-circle are \n\n$$\nx=6\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=6\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in \\left[0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right].\n$$" 13194139534687 false] [17592186087679 1020 #edb/ref 17592186303371 13194139534687 true] [17592186087679 1020 #edb/ref 17592186303385 13194139534687 true] [17592186087680 1020 #edb/ref 17592186303398 13194139534687 true] [17592186087725 1020 #edb/ref 17592186303392 13194139534687 true] [17592186087725 1020 #edb/ref 17592186303418 13194139534687 true] [17592186087725 1020 #edb/ref 17592186303425 13194139534687 true] [17592186087725 1020 #edb/ref 17592186303432 13194139534687 true] [17592186087727 1020 #edb/ref 17592186303378 13194139534687 true] [17592186087727 1020 #edb/ref 17592186303404 13194139534687 true] [17592186087727 1020 #edb/ref 17592186303411 13194139534687 true] [17592186088216 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534687 true] [17592186088216 1100 "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" 13194139534687 true] [17592186088216 1100 "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$x = 3\\cos \\theta , y = 3\\sin \\theta , 0 \\le \\theta  < 2\\pi $." 13194139534687 false] [17592186088216 1145 "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy squaring the $x$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {x}^{2} & =(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =9{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy squaring the $y$-equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {y}^{2} & =(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =9{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =9{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +9{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =9({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =9(1) \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9.$" 13194139534687 true] [17592186088298 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534687 true] [17592186088298 1100 "Which of the following parametric equations, defined for $\\theta  \\in  [0, 2\\pi ),$ generate a circle of radius $1$ centered at the point $(3, 2)$?" 13194139534687 false] [17592186088298 1100 "Which of the following parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate a circle of radius $1$ centered at the point $(3,2)?$" 13194139534687 true] [17592186088298 1145 "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +a,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +b,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $1$ centered at the point $(3,2).$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" 13194139534687 true] [17592186285409 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039602/assets/3c07f3e732084327f02849cee9355a46ce7a1c5f905d509b1e1404915612c85c.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $3$ centered at $(3,-2)$, as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(6,-2)?$" 13194139534687 true] [17592186285409 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/assets/3c07f3e732084327f02849cee9355a46ce7a1c5f905d509b1e1404915612c85c.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $3$ centered at $(3,-2)$, as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(6,-2)?$" 131941