metric equations of the three-quarter-circle are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}].\n$$" 13194139534687 false] [17592186303371 1017 #edb/ref 17592186303372 13194139534687 true] [17592186303371 1089 "q-106040" 13194139534687 true] [17592186303371 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534687 true] [17592186303371 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039602/assets/c36411be4d4fd094d93641a71721a22d1bbc040e15deeefff158915cc68ca52a.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $5$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(5,0)?$" 13194139534687 true] [17592186303371 1113 #uuid "4e330d13-b7fa-5110-8573-56d34299aa44" 13194139534687 true] [17592186303371 1145 "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$ centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r,0).$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=5.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" 13194139534687 true] [17592186303372 1000 #edb/ref 17592186303373 13194139534687 true] [17592186303372 1000 #edb/ref 17592186303374 13194139534687 true] [17592186303372 1000 #edb/ref 17592186303375 13194139534687 true] [17592186303372 1000 #edb/ref 17592186303376 13194139534687 true] [17592186303372 1000 #edb/ref 17592186303377 13194139534687 true] [17592186303372 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534687 true] [17592186303372 1041 "selection" 13194139534687 true] [17592186303372 1104 #edb/ref 17592186303377 13194139534687 true] [17592186303372 1110 #uuid "776b7cde-eb3a-56a1-a1ee-2446c783da96" 13194139534687 true] [17592186303373 1034 "x=25\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=25\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " 13194139534687 true] [17592186303373 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534687 true] [17592186303373 1111 #uuid "f46f62bd-7604-53be-a3b1-ec3eba683e82" 13194139534687 true] [17592186303374 1034 "x=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " 13194139534687 true] [17592186303374 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534687 true] [17592186303374 1111 #uuid "41be26dd-81a7-5087-8f03-f8029c54ad61" 13194139534687 true] [17592186303375 1034 "x=25\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=10\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " 13194139534687 true] [17592186303375 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534687 true] [17592186303375 1111 #uuid "7d3a27ec-134f-5bee-9e09-f888212bc57f" 13194139534687 true] [17592186303376 1034 "x=10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=10\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " 13194139534687 true] [17592186303376 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534687 true] [17592186303376 1111 #uuid "1113f6c1-5d17-593e-8040-29d140e56e56" 13194139534687 true] [17592186303377 1034 "x=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " 13194139534687 true] [17592186303377 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534687 true] [17592186303377 1111 #uuid "c4c4b4d4-1519-5898-a716-26de8e522c25" 13194139534687 true] [17592186303378 1017 #edb/ref 17592186303379 13194139534687 true] [17592186303378 1089 "q-106299" 13194139534687 true] [17592186303378 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534687 true] [17592186303378 1100 "Determine the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1,\\,y=\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" 13194139534687 true] [17592186303378 1113 #uuid "2ab0fea2-8c6c-59ec-a88b-21257097f620" 13194139534687 true] [17592186303378 1145 "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}(x+1) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{4}{9}{(x+1)}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+2 & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}(y+2) & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{4}{9}{(y+2)}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{9}{(x+1)}^{2}+\\frac{4}{9}{(y+2)}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 4{(x+1)}^{2}+4{(y+2)}^{2} & =9 \\\\ {(x+1)}^{2}+{(y+2)}^{2} & =\\frac{9}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation of the circle is ${(x+1)}^{2}+{(y+2)}^{2}=\\frac{9}{4}.$" 13194139534687 true] [17592186303379 1000 #edb/ref 17592186303380 13194139534687 true] [17592186303379 1000 #edb/ref 17592186303381 13194139534687 true] [17592186303379 1000 #edb/ref 17592186303382 13194139534687 true] [17592186303379 1000 #edb/ref 17592186303383 13194139534687 true] [17592186303379 1000 #edb/ref 17592186303384 13194139534687 true] [17592186303379 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534687 true] [17592186303379 1041 "selection" 13194139534687 true] [17592186303379 1104 #edb/ref 17592186303380 13194139534687 true] [17592186303379 1110 #uuid "6a8792f3-f6ae-5a7e-9d5a-96bb37db3af4" 13194139534687 true] [17592186303380 1034 "{(x+1)}^{2}+{(y+2)}^{2}=\\frac{9}{4}" 13194139534687 true] [17592186303380 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534687 true] [17592186303380 1111 #uuid "d9178563-1530-5722-836e-3e683572f035" 13194139534687 true] [17592186303381 1034 "{(x+1)}^{2}+{(y+2)}^{2}=\\frac{3}{2}" 13194139534687 true]