erence/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039602/assets/05a4ea8872b0e1197a248f412b4d1e51a170cc04b2da24aa7f6d05679985a74e.png)\n\nThe arc of the circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$ Assume $\\theta $ is between $0$ and $2\\pi .$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$]$" 13194139534687 true] [17592186303392 1113 #uuid "7914b9ee-25d9-568d-8f8e-0a493023d9f8" 13194139534687 true] [17592186303392 1145 "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in (0,2\\pi ].\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=3.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in (0,2\\pi ].\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to the arc above, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039602/assets/3f259a44786f9851588e212396f1c9fe3259a63c88b8c3090ebc6f1e50a6c456.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[\\pi ,2\\pi ]$, we get the required arc of the circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the arc of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [\\pi ,2\\pi ].\n$$" 13194139534687 true] [17592186303393 1000 #edb/ref 17592186303394 13194139534687 true] [17592186303393 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534687 true] [17592186303393 1041 "field-1" 13194139534687 true] [17592186303393 1104 #edb/ref 17592186303394 13194139534687 true] [17592186303393 1110 #uuid "60018cf2-e07f-5529-bae6-093443b27ff0" 13194139534687 true] [17592186303394 1034 "\\pi" 13194139534687 true] [17592186303394 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534687 true] [17592186303394 1111 #uuid "123729e9-f5d9-5267-a64f-8a2c739c1fbb" 13194139534687 true] [17592186303395 1000 #edb/ref 17592186303396 13194139534687 true] [17592186303395 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534687 true] [17592186303395 1041 "field-2" 13194139534687 true] [17592186303395 1104 #edb/ref 17592186303396 13194139534687 true] [17592186303395 1110 #uuid "30d59160-0947-5b77-bfa2-27a0458b0379" 13194139534687 true] [17592186303396 1034 "2\\pi" 13194139534687 true] [17592186303396 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534687 true] [17592186303396 1111 #uuid "66c1b636-95a7-5665-9890-0894f4d84ee5" 13194139534687 true] [17592186303397 1034 "10\\sin(\\theta)" 13194139534687 true] [17592186303397 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534687 true] [17592186303397 1111 #uuid "c0fc7a00-dad9-5bc1-8443-11e841f007c5" 13194139534687 true] [17592186303398 1017 #edb/ref 17592186303399 13194139534687 true] [17592186303398 1017 #edb/ref 17592186303402 13194139534687 true] [17592186303398 1089 "q-321942" 13194139534687 true] [17592186303398 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534687 true] [17592186303398 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039602/assets/59cef258a1ad0e6a0f56c37e0b7020e5341c329d5655c41f49e6e78eafca8b3f.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $6$ centered at $(-5,-2)$, as shown above. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ that traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(1,-2).$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$" 13194139534687 true] [17592186303398 1113 #uuid "67c0d567-0a98-5b60-aa47-f735cc300b15" 13194139534687 true] [17592186303398 1145 "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +a,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +b,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r+a,b).$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $6$ centered at the point $(-5,-2).$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are\n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -5,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" 13194139534687 true] [17592186303399 1000 #edb/ref 17592186303400 13194139534687 true] [17592186303399 1000 #edb/ref 17592186303401 13194139534687 true] [17592186303399 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534687 true] [17592186303399 1041 "field-1" 13194139534687 true] [17592186303399 1104 #edb/ref 17592186303401 13194139534687 true] [17592186303399 1110 #uuid "e719faf4-002d-516d-a797-2de8c8a4a279" 13194139534687 true] [17592186303400 1034 "6\\cos(\\theta)+5" 13194139534687 true] [17592186303400 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534687 true] [17592186303400 1111 #uuid "fd457ab8-0181-5b5e-ad2f-f482e00b683b" 13194139534687 true] [17592186303401 1034 "6\\cos(\\theta)-5" 13194139534687 true] [17592186303401 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534687 true] [17592186303401 1111 #uuid "659e564d-1f0e-55ea-a92d-07a5da294ed8" 13194139534687 true] [17592186303402 1000 #edb/ref 17592186303403 13194139534687 true] [17592186303402 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534687 true] [17592186303402 1041 "field-2" 13194139534687 true] [17592186303402 1104 #edb/ref 17592186303403 13194139534687 true] [17592186303402 1110 #uuid "c021c6cd-24a7-5256-b792-7421352b6137" 13194139534687 true] [17592186303403 1034 "6\\sin(\\theta)-2" 13194139534687 true] [17592186303403 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534687 true] [17592186303403 1111 #uuid "8b706664-71e1-5342-a88b-1e6072f8a3e3" 13194139534687 true] [17592186303404 1017 #edb/ref 17592186303405 13194139534687 true] [17592186303404 1017 #edb/ref 17592186303407 13194139534687 true] [17592186303404 1017 #edb/ref 17592186303409 13194139534687 true] [17592186303404 1089 "q-321988" 13194139534687 true] [17592186303404 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534687 true] [17592186303404 1100 "The Cartesian equation of the circle defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$(x\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}+(y\\,-$\n{{field-2}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-3}}." 13194139534687 true] [17592186303404 1113 #uuid "5ca6697a-5172-5e08-af3d-aff0cbcd8ea6" 13194139534687 true] [17592186303404 1145 "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+3 & =\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}(x+3) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{4}{25}{(x+3)}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}(y-1) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{4}{25}{(y-1)}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{25}{(x+3)}^{2}+\\frac{4}{25}{(y-1)}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{4}{25}{(x+3)}^{2}+\\frac{4}{25}{(y-1)}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {(x+3)}^{2}+{(y-1)}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation is ${(x+3)}^{2}+{(y-1)}^{2}=\\frac{25}{4}.$" 13194139534687 true] [17592186303405 1000 #edb/ref 17592186303406 13194139534687 true] [17592186303405 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534687 true] [17592186303405 1041 "field-1" 13194139534687 true] [17592186303405 1104 #edb/ref 17592186303406 13194139534687 true] [17592186303405 1110 #uuid "5d33d357